//! Two open premises become one.
//!
//! `bnd_res` and `over_res` differed in exactly one hypothesis — an exact length
//! equation versus a lower bound — and **both were used only to locate the
//! major**. Convert each to the slot form and the two hypothesis sets become
//! character-for-character identical; the collapsed premise never mentions spine
//! length at all. That is the evidence they genuinely unify, rather than being
//! two premises wearing one hat.
//!
//! The missing piece was slot-inhabitance — a list long enough to have a k-th
//! element has one. Only its converse existed.
//!
//! # What is left afterwards
//!
//! `hnf3`'s application residual now has ONE open premise: a delta-dead
//! recursor spine whose major sits in the major slot and is not a bound
//! variable. That is the stuck-major confluence argument, which
//! `stuck_recursor.rs` declares unattempted — the reachable majors of a reduct
//! are reducts of the MAJOR, not of its whnf.
//!
//! # Disjointness, checked for all three supplied rows
//!
//! A collapsed premise that silently absorbed an already-discharged row would be
//! a merge on paper only. So: it refutes under-application (via the slot
//! equation's own length consequence), and it is disjoint from both the boundary
//! bvar-major row and the over-applied bvar-slot row (slot content is unique).
//!
//! # One honest loss, recoverable
//!
//! `bnd_res` handed a future discharger the exact length, from which
//! under-application follows directly. The collapsed premise does not; the
//! discharger re-derives it. **Trap:** `nat_le_succ_or` takes `C : Prop` and is
//! unusable against an `nf_head` goal — `nat_le_trichotomy_t` takes `C : Type`
//! and is the one to use.
//!
//! `DerivedProved`, empty axiom closures.

use crate::spec::error::SpecError;
use crate::spec::Specification;

impl Specification {
    /// Slot-inhabitance, the disjointness checks, and the collapsed premise.
    pub(super) fn add_slot_collapse(&mut self) -> Result<(), SpecError> {
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_LIST_HEAD_DROP_OF_LE,
            "list_head_drop_of_le: THE MISSING SLOT-INHABITANCE LEMMA — a list long enough to have a k-th element has one. Only its converse existed. CPS at C : Type, because the consumer's goal is nf_head, which is Type-valued, while Le is Prop. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_LIST_HEAD_DROP_OF_LE_WITNESS,
            "list_head_drop_of_le_witness (NON-VACUITY, computational): runs the lemma and asserts by Eq.refl that it returns the right element. A stuck or wrong result fails to elaborate. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_BVAR_DISJOINT,
            "slot_bvar_disjoint: slot content is unique, so a non-bvar major and a bvar in the same slot are contradictory. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_UNDER_APPLIED_EMPTY,
            "slot_under_applied_empty: the slot equation refutes under-application. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_BND_BVAR_DISJOINT,
            "slot_bnd_bvar_disjoint: DISJOINTNESS from the boundary bvar-major row. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_OVER_BVAR_DISJOINT,
            "slot_over_bvar_disjoint: DISJOINTNESS from the over-applied bvar-slot row. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_HNF3_APP_RESIDUAL_SLOT_DISPATCH,
            "hnf3_app_residual_slot_dispatch: the nine-arm convoy on the recovered major, factored ONCE and shared by the boundary and over-applied branches. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_HNF3_APP_RESIDUAL_REC_SLOT_NARROWED,
            "hnf3_app_residual_rec_slot_narrowed: TWO OPEN PREMISES BECOME ONE. \\
\\
bnd_res and over_res differed in exactly one hypothesis — an exact length equation versus a lower bound — and BOTH were used only to LOCATE THE MAJOR. Converting each to the slot form makes the two hypothesis sets character-for-character identical, and the collapsed premise never mentions spine length at all. That is the evidence they genuinely unify rather than being two premises wearing one hat. \\
\\
The length premise is not smuggled back: list_head_drop_some_le_succ re-derives the bound from the slot equation, so carrying it would be redundant. \\
\\
ONE HONEST LOSS, and it is recoverable. bnd_res handed a future discharger the exact length, from which under-application follows directly. The collapsed premise does not, so the discharger re-derives it by nat_le_trichotomy_t and refutes the under-applied branch with slot_under_applied_empty. NOTE THE TRAP: nat_le_succ_or takes C : Prop and is UNUSABLE against an nf_head goal; nat_le_trichotomy_t takes C : Type and is the one to use. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_HNF3_APP_RESIDUAL_COVERED_SLOT_NARROWED,
            "hnf3_app_residual_covered_slot_narrowed: hnf3's application residual with ONE open premise. \\
\\
Every head shape is discharged except a delta-dead recursor spine whose major sits in the major slot and is not a bound variable. That single premise is now the whole remaining obligation, and it is the stuck-major confluence argument that stuck_recursor.rs declares unattempted. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_RES_BOUNDARY_SLOT_WITNESS,
            "slot_res_boundary_slot_witness (NON-VACUITY): at the real reflected Nat.rec, a 4-argument spine really does hold a non-bvar at slot MAJOR_IDX. Eq.refl only. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_RES_OVER_APPLIED_SLOT_WITNESS,
            "slot_res_over_applied_slot_witness (NON-VACUITY): and a 5-argument spine, where the major sits strictly inside. Eq.refl only. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_RES_BOUNDARY_WITNESS,
            "slot_res_boundary_witness: the collapsed premise applied in the boundary region. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        self.add_recursive_def(
            SRC_SLOT_RES_OVER_APPLIED_WITNESS,
            "slot_res_over_applied_witness: and in the over-applied region — so the merged premise is inhabited on BOTH sides of the merge, not just one. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.",
        )?;
        Ok(())
    }
}

